WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.110 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  presidential candidate and former rebel  gustavo petras galvanized millions  especially young voters by promising to  change this deeply unequal country  twenty-year-old stephanie ramirez  decided to volunteer for petra's  campaign after she was unable to pay for  her college during the pandemic and her  parents struggled to keep their small  shop open  it's been very difficult as a young  colombian to live in such precarious  conditions when it comes to work and  access to health and education and i  believe that petro is betting on  changing all of this  [Music]  last year she joined tens of thousands  of other young colombians to protest  against living conditions  they were some of the most violent  demonstrations colombia has seen in  decades  the police crackdown only inflamed the  discontent of those outraged by the  country's widening inequality  is before the protests had been very  inactive politically but with all the  injustices and killings i could not  remain indifferent it evoked the desire  for things to be different for us  peter is backed by many poor and working  class colombians but viewed with alarm  by businessmen and some middle class  voters they fear as plans to halt new  oil development or redistribute pension  funds could put economic stability at  risk  this letter sent by the manager of one  of the biggest milk companies in the  country urging suppliers not to vote for  petro went viral last week  it says petro would put at risk private  property and the freedom of enterprise  in the country a petrol clause has also  been added to a number of contracts this  year allowing the parties to scrap the  deal if the former geria comes to power  mario hernandez who runs one of the best  known colombian fashion houses is not  hiding his support for petra's main  rival federico gutierrez who is seen as  a continuation of business-friendly  policies of president ivan duque  i prefer the devil i know i've said it  many times in south america the left has  never been successful from cuba downward  that's the example  but with more than 20 million people  living in poverty and rising violence  polls show that 70 percent of colombians  want change not all of them believe  peter's proposals are the right ones but  enough people could decide to give him  and the left a chance alessandra  ampietti al jazeera bogota

